    "knowledge_point": "Finding Critical Points of Exponential Functions",
    "problem": "Find the critical points of the function  $f(x)=({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){e}^{4{x}^{2}}.$",
    "worked_solution": "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nDifferentiating $f(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){e}^{4{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4})}^{′}{e}^{4{x}^{2}}+({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){({e}^{4{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =2x{e}^{4{x}^{2}}+(8x)({x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}){e}^{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =[2x+8{x}^{3}-10x]{e}^{4{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(8{x}^{3}-8x){e}^{4{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n- First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(8{x}^{3}-8x){e}^{4{x}^{2}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{4{x}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we can divide this equation by ${e}^{4{x}^{2}}$ and solve the resulting equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}-8x & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 8x(x+1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,-1,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\n- Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x=-1,$ $x=0$ and $x=1.$",
